---
/**
 * The hero comparison: the same job asked of an MCP server and of `f`.
 *
 * WHY TWO PANES RATHER THAN A TABLE
 *
 * index.md already carries the per-capability table (5 tokens to write the call,
 * 260 of definition) and it should keep it -- that is the precise, quotable form.
 * What a table cannot show is shape. The argument is not "f is cheaper" in the
 * abstract; it is that the MCP request body is mostly schema and the `f` request
 * body is mostly the thing you asked for. Two panes of what actually goes over the
 * wire make that visible in the way a row of numbers does not.
 *
 * WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM
 *
 * Every figure is produced by docs/scripts/measure-comparison.mjs against a live
 * server (a real `tools/list` response from @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem,
 * 14 tools) and the committed snapshot from scripts/count-tokens.mjs for the `f` side,
 * both tokenized with o200k_base. Nothing here is typed by hand: re-run the script and
 * edit what it says. It reproduces the 260 tokens already published on
 * /docs/measurements/ for read_text_file, which is the check that this method matches
 * the audited one, and its 61 tokens / 16 ucks / 3.8 per uck are the same triple
 * /index.md quotes.
 *
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLAIMED
 *
 * The MCP pane is one server. Twenty servers cost more; that is the scaling
 * argument on /docs/measurements/, not this one. The `f` pane is not free either:
 * 54 tokens of index is paid once per session and it grows with the store. Both
 * numbers appear below so the comparison cannot be read as "f costs nothing".
 *
 * Also unstated because it is false: the MCP pane does not show the tool *result*,
 * and neither does the `f` pane show command output. Output is output on both
 * sides and is not what differs.
 */

// Produced by scripts/measure-comparison.mjs -- values there, not invented here.
const MCP = {
  server: "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem",
  tools: 14,
  resident: 2877,
  meanPerTool: 205.5,
  min: 153,
  max: 294,
  largestTool: "read_media_file",
  largestTokens: 294,
};

const F = {
  ucks: 16,
  indexOnce: 61,
  call: 5,
  perUckMean: 3.8,
};
---

<!-- Two panels of related content, not a data table. role="table" was wrong here: the
     bodies are code blocks and sentences rather than cells, so a screen reader would
     announce a grid of two columns with no rows and read the prose as cell values. As a
     list of two labelled items, the relationship (same job, two ways) is stated in the
     aria-label and each pane reads as itself. -->
<ol class="compare" aria-label="The same job, asked of an MCP server and of f">
  <li class="compare-col">
    <p class="compare-head">
      <span class="compare-svc">MCP</span>
      <span class="compare-sub">{MCP.server}</span>
    </p>
    <pre class="compare-body"><code>{`{
  "type": "tool_use",
  "name": "read_text_file",
  "input": { "path": "notes.md" }
}`}</code></pre>
    <p class="compare-foot">
      <b>{MCP.resident.toLocaleString()}</b> tokens resident in the body of every
      request — {MCP.tools} tools × {MCP.meanPerTool} mean, {MCP.min}–{MCP.max} each.
      {MCP.largestTokens} of that is <code>{MCP.largestTool}</code> alone
      (read_text_file, the tool compared below, is {260}).
    </p>
  </li>

  <li class="compare-col">
    <p class="compare-head">
      <span class="compare-svc f">f</span>
      <span class="compare-sub">{F.ucks} ucks in store</span>
    </p>
    <pre class="compare-body"><code>f creds get prod.db</code></pre>
    <p class="compare-foot">
      <b>{F.indexOnce}</b> tokens, once, for all {F.ucks} command names
      ({F.perUckMean} each). Between invocations: nothing serialized.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
